NNAAMMEE
       pampaintspill - smoothly spill colors into the background


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll  [----bbggccoolloorr=_c_o_l_o_r] [----wwrraapp] [----aallll] [----ddoowwnnssaammppllee=_n_u_m_b_e_r]
       [----nneeaarr=_n_u_m_b_e_r] [----ppoowweerr=_n_u_m_b_e_r] [_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e] [--rraannddoommsseeeedd==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r]

       Minimum unique abbreviations of option are  acceptable.   You  may  use
       double hyphens instead of single hyphen to denote options.  You may use
       white space in place of the equals sign to separate an option name from
       its value.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll  produces  a  smooth  color gradient from all of the non-
       background-colored pixels in  an  input  image,  effectively  "spilling
       paint"  onto  the  background.  ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll is similar to ppaammggrraaddiieenntt
       but differs in the following characteristics:



       •      ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll accepts any number of paint
                    sources (non-background-colored  pixels),  which  can  lie
              anywhere
                    on the canvas.  ppaammggrraaddiieenntt accepts exactly
                    four paint sources, one in each corner of the image.


       •      ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll requires an input image while
                    ppaammggrraaddiieenntt generates a new image from
                    scratch.


       •      ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll can produce tileable output and
                    can  control how tightly the gradient colors bind to their
              source
                    pixels.


       Results are generally best when the input image contains  just  a  few,
       crisp  spots  of color. Use your drawing program's pencil tool - as op‐
       posed to a paintbrush or airbrush tool - with a small nib.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll recognizes
       the following command line options:



       ----bbggccoolloorr=_c_o_l_o_r

              Explicitly specify the background color. _c_o_l_o_r can be
                    specified  using  any  of  the  formats  accepted  by  the
              pnm_parsecolor()              library                    routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩  such as red or #ff0000.  If
                    ----bbggccoolloorr is not specified, ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll makes an
                    educated guess about the background  color  based  on  the
              colors in the
                    image's corners.


       ----wwrraapp

              Allow gradients to wrap around image borders. That is, colors
                    that spill off the right side of the image reappear on the
              left side of
                    the image and likewise for left/right, top/bottom, and
                    bottom/top. ----wwrraapp makes images tileable,  which  is  nice
              for
                    producing desktop backgrounds.


       ----aallll

              Recolor all pixels, not just background pixels. Normally,
                    non-background-colored  pixels  in  the input image appear
              unmodified in
                    the output image. With ----aallll, _a_l_l pixels are colored
                    based on their distance from all of the (other)  non-back‐
              ground-colored
                    pixels.


       ----ddoowwnnssaammppllee=_n_u_m_b_e_r

              Ignore all but _n_u_m_b_e_r non-background-colored pixels.
                    When a large number of pixels in the input image differ in
              color from
                    the background, ppaammppaaiinnttssppiillll runs very slowly. The
                    ----ddoowwnnssaammppllee option randomly selects  a  given  number  of
              colored
                    pixels  to  use as paint sources for the gradients and ig‐
              nores the rest,
                    thereby trading off image quality for speed of execution.


       ----nneeaarr=_n_u_m_b_e_r

              Consider only the nearest _n_u_m_b_e_r paint sources when computing
                    a pixel's new color.  The default is to consider all paint
              sources.
                    In  most  cases, _n_u_m_b_e_r should be fairly small, or its im‐
              pact
                    will be minimal and execution time will increase  unneces‐
              sarily.  A
                    value  of  1  produces  a coloring that looks a lot like a
              Voronoi
                    diagram.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.97 (December 2021).


       ----ppoowweerr=_n_u_m_b_e_r

              Control how color intensity changes as a function of the
                    distance from a paint source. The default value for _n_u_m_b_e_r
              is
                    -2.0, which means that intensity drops (because of the mi‐
              nus sign) with
                    the square (because of the 2.0) of the distance from  each
              paint
                    source.  -2.0  generally works well in practice, but other
              values can be
                    specified for various special  effects.  With  very  small
              numbers of paint
                    sources, -1.0 may produce subtler gradients, but these get
              muddier as
                    the number of paint sources  increases.  Positive  numbers
              (e.g., 1.0 and
                    2.0)  make the paint sources stand out in the output image
              by pushing the
                    gradients away from them.


       --rraannddoommsseeeedd==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r

              This is the seed for the random number generator that  generates
              the
                pixels.

              Use  this  to  ensure you get the same image on separate invoca‐
              tions.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.94 (March 2021).




